  This presentation is a sequel to the one with the same name presented at
  \acro{TUG} 2025. The previous talk described an archival technique using \TeX,
  but without the help of \TeX4ht owing to its inability to process \tbUTF-8
  content (then). Now that \TeX4ht can digest \tbUTF-8 content, we have revised
  the workflow to make use of the newer capabilities, including math
  processing.

  In the revised workflow, we employ \TeX4ht to process the \TeX{} sources to
  generate an enriched \acro{HTML} output which in turn is used as the source to
  create \acro{XML} output. We adopted this deviation from the traditional
  procedure (\TeX{}$\rightarrow$ \acro{XML}$\rightarrow$ \acro{HTML})
  due to the practical consideration of using a volunteer workforce with lesser
  expertise in markup formats, who find it difficult to fix parse
  errors while validating the \acro{XML} output by editing the \TeX{} sources.

  The current process splits the workload between the two categories of average
  volunteers and (scarcely available) expert ones, the latter being engaged
  solely for \XML\ production. The talk provides more information about the
  aforementioned workflow and related technologies.
